course: dictionary
lesson: 9602
title: Strictly: firm, exact and no exceptions
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: On a sign, «Swimming strictly forbidden» means swimming is never allowed there. The word strictly makes the rule firm and clear. Strictly is an adverb, so it tells you how something is done or how far it goes.
explain: Strictly has two main meanings. It can mean in a firm way that makes sure rules are followed exactly, as in «Japan's Tokugawa rulers strictly limited contact with foreigners». It can also mean completely, with no exceptions. Read the words around it to decide which meaning fits.
explain: «Strictly speaking, a tomato is a fruit» means if you are being very exact about the meaning. In maths, «strictly greater than» means bigger and never equal. Ask yourself: is this a rule, a sign, a sum, or a careful correction?
fact: Strictly is an adverb, so it often tells you how something happens.
fact: «Strictly» means in a firm way that makes sure rules are followed exactly.
fact: «Strictly» can also mean completely or exactly, with no exceptions.
fact: «Strictly speaking» means if you are being very exact about the meaning.
fact: In maths, «strictly greater than» means bigger and not equal, like the > sign.
exercise: «Swimming strictly forbidden.» What does strictly mean on this sign? | *It is never allowed, with no exceptions | It is allowed if you ask nicely | It is a little bit forbidden | It is allowed on Sundays
exercise: In maths, what does «strictly greater than» mean? | Roughly the same as | Smaller than | Equal to | *Bigger and not equal
exercise: «Strictly speaking, a tomato is a fruit.» What does strictly speaking mean here? | If you are shouting loudly | If you are guessing | If you are telling a joke | *If you are being very exact about the meaning
test: In old English, «sternly» meant the same as strictly. What does sternly mean? | Happily | *In a firm, stern way | Politely | Quickly
test: «In Essex, where screens and sweets are strictly limited, Albie eats a whole bag of sweets.» What does strictly limited suggest? | *Kept under firm control, with rules followed closely | Made into a game | Given away free to everyone | Forgotten by everyone
exam: «Monkeys ... in many countries keeping them as pets is banned or strictly controlled.» What does strictly controlled mean? | *Kept under firm rules and closely watched | Given away free in shops | Sold to anyone who asks | Left alone with no rules at all
